1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,617
Hi I'm Edi and this is QPlace.

2
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:08,267
So sometimes we need to take a server down to fix it and in Kubernetes we call that draining

3
00:00:08,267 --> 00:00:09,050
a node.

4
00:00:09,267 --> 00:00:14,533
So every app on that node we call them pods and those pods and basically those apps have

5
00:00:14,533 --> 00:00:18,183
to move to another node first before we take it down and fix it.

6
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:23,300
So the thing is in each node, each node only has like so much room and some pods can only

7
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:24,867
run on some nodes.

8
00:00:24,867 --> 00:00:27,583
So before you start bringing down your server to fix it,

9
00:00:27,667 --> 00:00:33,283
you need to ask yourself the question you want to know will every app fit on this new node

10
00:00:34,067 --> 00:00:36,000
Here's a small example right?

11
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,700
Two pods, two nodes.

12
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:40,467
Each node has room for 2.5 CPUs.

13
00:00:40,467 --> 00:00:43,567
Now the batch pod itself needs two CPUs.

14
00:00:43,567 --> 00:00:50,767
The indexer pod needs 1.5, and it can only run on node A because node A has the fast disk.

15
00:00:50,767 --> 00:00:55,267
The easy way is to take the biggest pod first and put it on the first node with room,

16
00:00:55,267 --> 00:00:57,733
so batch goes on node A.

17
00:00:57,733 --> 00:01:00,400
Now node A only has half a CPU left.

18
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:04,267
Indexer doesn't fit there, and node B won't take you know indexer itself.

19
00:01:04,267 --> 00:01:09,150
So the easy way is to move one pod out of the two.

20
00:01:10,967 --> 00:01:13,117
But there's a way to actually fit both, right?

21
00:01:13,333 --> 00:01:20,333
If you put batch on node B and indexer on node A, our normal solver tries every option and

22
00:01:20,333 --> 00:01:21,150
finds that.

23
00:01:21,233 --> 00:01:23,717
Then a separate checker goes over the plan.

24
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,817
If each pod on a node is allowed on, does each node have enough room?

25
00:01:29,967 --> 00:01:31,417
Now the quantum part.

26
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:35,317
Each choice, like batch goes to node A, becomes one qubit.

27
00:01:35,467 --> 00:01:37,617
Here there are three choices, so three qubits.

28
00:01:37,967 --> 00:01:43,233
We give each plan a score, it gets points for every pod it moves and loses a lot of points

29
00:01:43,233 --> 00:01:47,917
if it breaks a rule, like putting one pod in two places or over filling a node.

30
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,633
Before we run anything, we try all 8 plans to make sure the best score really is the best

31
00:01:52,633 --> 00:01:53,083
plan.

32
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:55,850
Then we run a quantum method called QAOA.

33
00:01:56,133 --> 00:01:59,750
Each time we read the qubits, we get back one plan.

34
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,683
Here it is running on Qollab.

35
00:02:02,733 --> 00:02:03,783
It sends one job.

36
00:02:03,967 --> 00:02:09,667
When I ran it before, out of 256 shots or 256 tries, the built-in simulator gave the full

37
00:02:09,667 --> 00:02:11,183
plan 84 times.

38
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:15,783
IonQ simulator, which copies the noise of the Aria 1 machine,

39
00:02:16,033 --> 00:02:17,617
gave it 83 times.

40
00:02:17,967 --> 00:02:21,083
So, if you just guess, you would get about one time in eight,

41
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:22,683
so about, you know, 32 times.

42
00:02:23,033 --> 00:02:27,333
So, basically, we check every answer, if one breaks a rule, we count it and throw it away.

43
00:02:27,333 --> 00:02:28,183
We don't fix it up.

44
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:34,333
Now, the normal computer still wins here, it's faster and it can prove it's found the best

45
00:02:34,333 --> 00:02:36,617
plan, so we are not saying quantum is better.

46
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:39,583
What we built works end to end, it reads a saved copy of a cluster,

47
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:43,417
gives you back a plan that has been checked with the normal answers next to it to compare,

48
00:02:43,633 --> 00:02:45,733
it only reads, it never changes the cluster.

49
00:02:45,733 --> 00:02:50,800
The example here is made up, the code is on GitHub, and I basically built all of these

50
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:52,850
things using Qollab and IonQ, so thanks for watching.

